[Music]  what's up everybody michael here now it  shouldn't be a surprise  that we're obsessed with adult animation  and with how seemingly silly shows like  rick and morty south park and bojack  horseman can actually teach us a lot  about some pretty serious stuff from  depression to gentrification to  emotionally processing that your mom  might be a clone but we've yet to touch  on the adult animation show that's not  afraid to throw some meat on the grill  king of the hill this slice of tex and  suburban life ran for over a decade in  the late 90s and early 2000s and while  it holds a special place in the hearts  of its fans it hasn't commanded the same  grip on the cultural conversation as its  contemporaries like the simpsons or  family guy and that may be because the  hill family and their neighbors aren't  often exploring lofty philosophical  ideas over their cold cans of american  lager there's no alcoholic grandpa to  give us nihilistic musings on the  universe and none of the episodes end  with someone saying but i've learned  something today in fact the show's  creator mike judge has said that he  specifically avoids injecting any  political messaging into his work we'd  argue that office space is definitely a  political film but who are we to  disagree with mr judge i'm tired of  being pushed around  aren't you yes peter but i'm not going  to do anything illegal illegal samir  this is america but does that mean that  the show is completely apolitical is it  even possible for a television show to  skirt politics entirely after all  kingdom health's characters are often  thrown into very political situations  and their world views are shaped by  their experiences so i see you finally  got luanne involved in politics that's  good  ass  the paper united stand  tommy go home and it's safe to say there  are very few television characters who  are as consistent and steadfast in their  values as hank hill what if somebody  wants theirs well done we ask them  politely yet firmly to leave it's easy  to read the show simply as a caricature  of conservative families in texas and  plenty of people do but is there a  deeper message hidden in the musings of  these working-class sweethearts let's  find out on this wisecrack edition on  king of the hill deep or dumb for the  uninitiated or forgetful king of the  hill depicts a fictional central texas  suburb called arlen and specifically the  hill family consisting of hank peggy and  their son bobby hank is an all-american  man's man he was a teen football star  married his high school sweetheart and  settled down in his hometown unlike  other animated sitcom dads with names  like homer or peter hank isn't portrayed  as an oafish slob who's barely keeping  his family intact instead he's a  principled loving patriarch who serves  as a moral center not only for his  family but also for his community he  believes in hard work family values and  of course the supremacy of propane over  any other fuel source you brought  charcoal into our house i didn't know  what it was luanne asked me to hold it  for her i thought it was drugs much of  the central conflict of the show comes  from hank's stalwart ways clashing  against the world's changing social and  political landscape conflicts can ensue  from everything from sex education in  public schools what sexual education  where'd you get this to eco-friendly  toilets  still that's not to say that hank is  overtly conservative at the very least  he seems to believe in climate change  it's already 110 in the summer and if it  gets one degree hotter i'm gonna kick  your ass and he acknowledges that  americans stole their land from  indigenous peoples i agree that stealing  indians land was wrong can't you agree  that eating people is wrong it may be  this moderate political positioning that  makes hank such an endearing character  to those all over the political spectrum  and why some critics have called king of  the hill the last bipartisan tv comedy  so is the show trying to extol the  values of moderate conservatism well not  exactly hank's behavior isn't guided by  his political leanings or his religious  beliefs even though he is a god-fearing  man i know god's up there and he knows  i'm smack dab in the middle of the  second pew instead he tends to make  decisions based on a combination of  conventional wisdom and an innate sense  of right and wrong now this doesn't  always lead to the correct decision in  fact hank nearly misses out on voting in  a presidential election because his  candidate had a flimsy  god  his handshake  it's limp as many times as hank is  portrayed as his family and friend's  moral guide he is just as often put in  situations where he has to compromise  some of these values in order to live a  fulfilling life and care for his family  this is especially true when it comes to  his relationship with his son bobby that  boy ain't right despite hank's best  efforts to pass down his brand of  masculinity bobby is most fulfilled when  he's performing prop comedy or stage  magic or ventriloquism his attempts to  push bobby into sports or other more  masculine coded pursuits only  deteriorates their relationship these  moments often cause hank to reflect on  his relationship with his own father who  he begins to realize might not have been  the best role model you know how i was  raised what my father's like  i got my shin blown off by japan man's  machine gun so don't come crying to me  with your problem most of these episodes  end with hank feeling content that at  least bobby has found his own niche in  society even if that means taking on  roles that hank considers more feminine  like when bobby shows an aptitude for  cooking and cleaning after taking a home  economics class if bobby learns to cook  and clean for himself what's his  motivation to ever get married this  speaks to what may be the underlying  theme of the whole series time and time  again hank's black and white values and  sense of identity are tested against the  tides of an increasingly complex and  diversified world which begs the  question what value do our political and  cultural ideologies have if they don't  help us function in our day-to-day lives  to find out we need to examine the work  of someone that hank hill would probably  ask to leave his barbecue but whose  ideas he might ultimately agree with  henry lefebvre was a french  marxist  philosopher  who also likely preferred a nice glass  of room temperature red wine to an ice  cold bud in his work the critique of  everyday life lefeb tried to bring  marxist theory into the experiences of  everyday working class people he became  increasingly frustrated with  intellectuals who seemed more interested  in pontificating about the values of the  working class than actually listening to  the experiences of workers he used the  example of an urban intellectual  strolling the french countryside  lamenting the plight of the peasant  farmer without ever experiencing the  hard labor it takes to work the land or  the centuries of cultivation it took to  shape it because the everyday is where  most life happens it's a sight of  transformative conflict worth taking  seriously more than anything he thought  that because capitalism is constantly  reproducing itself via the everyday  revolutionizing our day-to-day existence  was a precondition for any type of  future utopian society now hank is  definitely not a marxist i hate  communists all they do is boss people  around sounds like you uncle hank but he  is a working-class guy whose livelihood  is often put in jeopardy by the forces  of capitalism he swears undying loyalty  to his boss and mentor buck strickland  even though he delegates most of the  actual work of running the shop to hank  while he spends his time drinking  gambling and philandering hank only  stands up to his boss when his immoral  ways threaten to rub off on bobby but  this isn't the only time hank struggles  against the forces of the free market  when the corporate superstore megalomart  threatens to put strickland propane out  of business hank laments the takeover of  local economies by these corporate  behemoths the only place you can find a  main street these days is in disneyland  just try to buy a gun there and when  that same superstore threatens the  quality of the town's meat supply he  even goes so far as to join a hippie  infested co-op just to get quality  stakes it's clear that hank is willing  to put aside his conservative reputation  when it comes to meeting the everyday  needs of his family and community when a  major back injury keeps him away from  work he not only applies for workers  compensation workers comp do i look like  a hobo to you he even joins a yoga  studio yoga  isn't that a cult above all hank values  hard work and while this may sometimes  blind him to how much his own labor is  being exploited by his boss it also  makes him keenly aware of the needs of  other working-class folks in his  community these people may have a  multitude of ideological differences but  at the end of the day they have more in  common with each other than they do with  megalomart or other external forces like  the government for example when the  public schools are forced to teach sex  education what do you know about sexual  relations  i don't know  nothing much  i'm a little worried about being a [ __ ]  while these forces may initially sway  some residents to their side by the end  of the episode it's the sense of  community that wins out and brings the  characters back together like when peggy  makes hella banks selling homes to  hipsters who want to live in a diverse  neighborhood but eventually she realizes  that she's been contributing to the  gentrification that almost forced hank's  co-worker enrique out of his home so she  remedies the situation i own my own  house i am a homeowner now i pay  mortgage instead of rent left might  describe this as the people of arlen  exerting their right to the city  according to him urban space was not the  product of the private entity who may  own it or even the state authority who  may govern it instead it is the product  of the people who inhabit the space the  food being cooked the language is being  spoken and the cultures being displayed  are all part of what makes an urban or  even suburban space thrive while cities  and neighborhoods may be built with the  backing of capital interests and ruled  over by local and federal governments  above all they're social experiments and  while the people of ireland aren't often  seen squatting in vacant buildings or  chaining themselves to construction  equipment although they do occasionally  engage in direct action they assert  their own right to the city in many  different ways these include legal  methods like when hank joins the zoning  board to repeal a ban on high flow  toilets and some less than legal methods  like when the residents tear down a  prefab house in their neighborhood when  its structural integrity threatens their  own homes while the majority of the  population of ireland has key  demographics in common the protagonists  of the show are not in the business of  imposing their world view onto the town  as a whole they simply want to be able  to live their lives in the way that best  suits them while also looking out for  the collective good of the community  they may scoff at environmentalist  hippies or poke fun at gay rodeo clowns  but they ultimately want to create a  space where all people can live their  best lives a lot has changed since king  of the hill went off the air the  political climate in the country has  become just a tad bit more polarized and  with the ever-growing influence of  social media political identities are  being formed in an arena that is far  removed from everyday in-person  interactions it's never been easier to  avoid your cousin jason at family  functions because he spammed you with  flat earther videos in your dms when you  don't have to physically interact with  someone with differing viewpoints it's  much easier to write them off and this  is precisely where we can still glean  some wisdom from king of the hill  because at first glance it's easy to  assume the show's message is the  importance of putting aside our  preconceived notions about people we  don't know and then working with them to  create communities that benefit us all  and on its face it may seem like this  finding common ground narrative is pure  political fantasy but what the hill  family and their neighbors show us is  that our everyday beliefs matter in big  ways so while we might think of  exploring various political ideologies  online as a type of cosplay it can have  real effects on our personal lives and  ultimately upon our wider communities  it's not an either or between the  everyday or the political but of both  and in which these spheres are  inextricably linked which means that if  we're open-minded and kind to our fellow  redditors we're helping to shape a  kinder world offline but if we're being  vengeful ideological dicks on social  media we're creating an echo chamber  that encourages a vibe of vengeful  dickishness to spread its wings in the  real world overall king of the hills  focus on the politics of everyday life  and the way it shows us how to use these  politics to assert our right to our own  spaces earns it a wisecrack certified  label of pretty deep but what do you all  think are we reading too much into a  show that spends a significant amount of  its air time on scenes like this yep  yep  yep or can the lives of these animated  texans teach us all how to live simpler  yet more fulfilling lives let us know in  the comments and thanks as always to our  amazing patrons for all your support and  don't forget to check out our new  updated patreon page also be sure to  check out our new stream wisecrack live  right here every thursday smash that  subscribe button like it's an empty can  of alamo beer and don't forget to ring  that bell and as always thanks for  watching later  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  you

